meta {
  name: 9 Delete account (domain half)
  type: http
  seq: 9
}
delete {
  url: {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/providers/{{providerEmail}}
  body: none
}
docs {
  ⚠️ **DESTRUCTIVE, and irreversible.** Erases the provider profile — services and embedded appointment list
  included — and scrubs the address out of their customers' records.

  ⚠️ **This is NOT `POST /api/v1/providers/{email}/deactivate`.** Deactivation sets `isActive=false`, hides
  the provider from the customer directory and **keeps everything**; it is reversible and is what a provider
  going temporarily offline wants. This deletes.

  **This is the domain half only. Call `0-Auth/10 Delete account (credential half)` SECOND** — the
  credential that route deletes is what authorises this one.

  What happens:
  - The `ProviderEntity` is deleted, taking its `services` and embedded `appointments` with it.
  - The provider's own notifications are deleted, and their session **notes** are deleted outright —
    those are private to them and are *about* their clients, so anonymising the owner would leave the
    client's free text standing with no owner and no route to erase it.
  - The device-token registration is deleted.
  - Appointments, messages and payments **survive** with the provider's address replaced by a tombstone
    (`deleted-user-xxxxxxxx@deleted.invalid`): a customer's own history of sessions they attended is theirs.
  - Every customer's subscription to this provider is `$pull`ed.

  Answers:
  - `204` on success — **and also when there was no profile to remove** (App Review Guideline 5.1.1(v)).
  - `403` for another provider's email. Ownership-guarded.
  - `500` if the erasure could not complete; nothing is removed, and the call is safe to retry.

  Idempotent.
}
